<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Theoretical Analysis</span></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present theoretical analysis, examine different aspects, and discuss convergence.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Lipschitz Continuity)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The objective functions for COBYLA maintain the Lipschitz continuity. This assumption ensures that the linear approximations employed by COBYLA remain precise within the trust region, contributing to a more reliable and quicker convergence.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Uniform <math id="Thmassumption2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smoothness)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The functions <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{F_{1},\dots,F_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{F_{1},\dots,F_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are uniformly <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth for <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. For arbitrary vectors <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, the following inequality is satisfied:</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{k}(y)\leq F_{k}(x)+\langle y-x,\nabla F_{k}(x)\rangle+\frac{L}{2}\|y-x\|^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k}(y)\leq F_{k}(x)+\langle y-x,\nabla F_{k}(x)\rangle+\frac{L}{2}\|y-x\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\{1,2,\dots,N\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\{1,2,\dots,N\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which serves as a quadratic upper bound for an <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth function.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3</span></span><span id="Thmassumption3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (<math id="Thmassumption3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-strong Convexity)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Functions <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{F_{1},\cdots,F_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{F_{1},\cdots,F_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are all <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-strongly convex: for all <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F_{k}(x)\geq F_{k}(y)+(x-y)^{T}\nabla F_{k}(y)+\frac{\mu}{2}\|x-y\|_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k}(x)\geq F_{k}(y)+(x-y)^{T}\nabla F_{k}(y)+\frac{\mu}{2}\|x-y\|_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 4</span></span><span id="Thmassumption4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Data Distribution Similarity)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a set of <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> clients participating, denoted as <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{C_{1},C_{2},\dots,C_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{C_{1},C_{2},\dots,C_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume that the data distributions of the data sets of these clients, represented by probability distributions </span>{<math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1},P_{2},\dots,P_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1},P_{2},\dots,P_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>}<span id="Thmassumption4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, exhibit a degree of similarity such that</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall i,j\in\{1,2,\dots,K\},\quad\delta(P_{i},P_{j})\leq\epsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i,j\in\{1,2,\dots,K\},\quad\delta(P_{i},P_{j})\leq\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmassumption4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmassumption4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> denotes a divergence measure (e.g., total variation distance, Kullback-Leibler divergence) between the distributions and <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is a small positive constant representing the maximum divergence allowed between any pair of distributions.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 5</span></span><span id="Thmassumption5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Device and Environmental Conditions)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Some assumptions we consider in terms of device and environmental conditions are as follows:</span></p>
<ol id="S6.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The devices are fully functional during local training without any failure.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Environmental factors such as rain, temperature, ions, etc., are considered to be ideal for the experimental simulation.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For experimental purposes, we assume that line of sight is visible for successful communication between satellite and ground stations (GS) or between satellite to satellite, i.e., after each device training, the device immediately sends the model to another device without any issue.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be a continuous Lipschitz function with Lipschitz constant <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. The regret <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(T)</annotation></semantics></math> of the COBYLA optimizer
after <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> iterations is limited by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite></span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{F}(T)=\sum_{k=1}^{T}[F(\theta_{t})-F(\theta^{*})]\leq L\sum_{t=1}^{T}\Delta_{t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{F}(T)=\sum_{k=1}^{T}[F(\theta_{t})-F(\theta^{*})]\leq L\sum_{t=1}^{T}\Delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where,
<math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the position at iteration <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the best solution, and <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the trust region radius at iteration <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The error limit at iteration <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> can be explained in terms of the trust region radius <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the precision of the linear approximations.
With the bound on the trust region radius,</p>
<table id="S6.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\theta_{t+1}-\theta^{*}\|\leq\Delta_{t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\theta_{t+1}-\theta^{*}\|\leq\Delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the best solution and <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the trust region radius at iteration <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
With a bound on the objective function error,</p>
<table id="S6.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f(\theta_{t+1})-f(\theta^{*})|\leq C\Delta_{t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f(\theta_{t+1})-f(\theta^{*})|\leq C\Delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant dependent on the Lipschitz continuity of the objective function <math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and the accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let Assumptions 1 to 5 hold, with strongly convex local objectives,
<math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> trust region radius adjustments at time <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>,
the expected difference between the value of the function in the model <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and the optimal value of the function <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\theta^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\theta^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite></span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E\left[F(\theta^{R})-F(\theta^{*})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E\left[F(\theta^{R})-F(\theta^{*})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\underbrace{L\sum_{t=1}^{T}\Delta_{t}}_{\text{Optimizer}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Optimizer</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\underbrace{L\sum_{t=1}^{T}\Delta_{t}}_{\text{Optimizer}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\underbrace{\mu(\theta_{0}-\theta^{*})^{2}\exp\left(-\frac{\mu\Delta_{t}R}{2}\right)}_{\text{FL}}\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">FL</mtext></munder></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\underbrace{\mu(\theta_{0}-\theta^{*})^{2}\exp\left(-\frac{\mu\Delta_{t}R}{2}\right)}_{\text{FL}}\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\frac{\Delta_{t}}{NK}+\frac{L\Delta_{t}^{2}}{NK}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\frac{\Delta_{t}}{NK}+\frac{L\Delta_{t}^{2}}{NK}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\underbrace{\gamma_{c}\tau_{c}R+\delta_{c}\rho_{loss}\rho+\epsilon_{c}\frac{\rho}{B}T}_{\text{SATCOM Factors}}\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mi>B</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">SATCOM Factors</mtext></munder></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\underbrace{\gamma_{c}\tau_{c}R+\delta_{c}\rho_{loss}\rho+\epsilon_{c}\frac{\rho}{B}T}_{\text{SATCOM Factors}}\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\underbrace{\alpha_{q}\sigma_{q}^{2}N_{q}}_{\text{Quantum Factor}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Quantum Factor</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\underbrace{\alpha_{q}\sigma_{q}^{2}N_{q}}_{\text{Quantum Factor}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where,
<math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the model parameters after rounds <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> satellites with <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> local updates, <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is the global objective function, <math id="S6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the optimal solution of the global objective function,
In addition,
<math id="S6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{c}\tau_{c}R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{c}\tau_{c}R</annotation></semantics></math> is impact of communication latency, where <math id="S6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant, <math id="S6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is the latency;
<math id="S6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{c}\rho_{loss}\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{c}\rho_{loss}\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is impact of packet loss, where <math id="S6.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant, <math id="S6.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{loss}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{loss}</annotation></semantics></math> is the packet loss rate, and <math id="S6.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is the amount of data transmitted;
<math id="S6.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{c}\frac{\rho}{B}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mi>B</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{c}\frac{\rho}{B}T</annotation></semantics></math> is impact of bandwidth constraints, where <math id="S6.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant, <math id="S6.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is the amount of data transmitted, <math id="S6.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the bandwidth and <math id="S6.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the duration of communication.
Whereas,
<math id="S6.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{q}\sigma_{q}^{2}N_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{q}\sigma_{q}^{2}N_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is impact of quantum noise, where <math id="S6.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant, <math id="S6.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is the noise level, and <math id="S6.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of qubits.
The proofs are provided in the appendix <a href="#S9" title="IX APPENDIX ‣ orb-QFL: Orbital Quantum Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IX</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VII </span><span id="S7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiment</span></h2>

<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VII-A</span> </span><span id="S7.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Set up &amp; Parameters.</span></h3>

<section id="S7.SS1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">LEO Constellation.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We experiment with <math id="S7.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> satellites in an LEO constellation that are equidistant from each other, covering the entire Earth, each in its orbit around the Earth in two different sets of experiments.
Each orbit of the satellite is located at a height of <math id="S7.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">500</annotation></semantics></math> km above the Earth’s surface, with an inclination angle of <math id="S7.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>60</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60</annotation></semantics></math> deg and an angular spacing of <math id="S7.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="360/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>360</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">360/n</annotation></semantics></math>.
For only a QFL setting, a geostationary satellite is considered which acts as a server.
For which, we experimented with <math id="S7.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> settings: the first geostationary satellite was located on Earth at about 20 m altitude; the second geostationary satellite is an actual GEO satellite with an altitude of <math id="S7.SS1.SSSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="35786*u.km" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>35786</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35786*u.km</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">Tools.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Matlab satellite communications toolbox</span> is used to simulate, analyze, and test satellite communications systems and links.
For performance simulation, the <span id="S7.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qiskit framework</span> is used, while for satellite simulation, the <span id="S7.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Poliastro library</span> is used.
Poliastro is a Python-based library that can be used for interactive astrodynamics and orbital mechanics.
Thus, we used the library to create satellite orbit paths and track their propagation, through which we keep a record of the distance between the satellites.
Qiskit is an open-source SDK for working with quantum computers at the levels of quantum circuits, operators, and primitives.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsubsection">Dataset Used.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For our experimental simulation, we used the Statlog (Landsat Satellite) dataset
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.
Statlog data are a multivariate data set that consists of multispectral values of pixels in <math id="S7.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> x <math id="S7.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> neighborhoods in satellite images.
In total, it has <math id="S7.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6435" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6435</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6435</annotation></semantics></math> samples with 36 features and <math id="S7.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> labels from <math id="S7.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S7.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> where each number relates to red soil, cotton crop, grey soil, damp gray soil, soil with vegetation stubble, mixture class and very damp grey soil, respectively.
Figure <a href="#S9.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ IX-C Statlog Dataset. ‣ IX APPENDIX ‣ orb-QFL: Orbital Quantum Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, shows the statlog datasets after reducing the dimension of the feature.
<math id="S7.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the dataset is used for training, while the rest is used for testing.
The data set is also distributed among varied numbers of devices in different sets of experiments of <math id="S7.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S7.SS1.SSSx3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> devices.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">VII-B</span> </span><span id="S7.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Results.</span></h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy.</span>
To evaluate the performance of the model at the end of each communication round, a hypothetical server is added to orb-QFL only for testing purposes.
From Figures <a href="#S7.F4.sf1" title="In Fig. 4 ‣ VII-B Results. ‣ VII Experiment ‣ orb-QFL: Orbital Quantum Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a> and <a href="#S7.F4.sf2" title="In Fig. 4 ‣ VII-B Results. ‣ VII Experiment ‣ orb-QFL: Orbital Quantum Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a>, we can see that orb-QFL performs better in terms of server performance.
This implies that the proposed method is better suited in terms of both training and test performance.
It is promising to see better performance considering we have omitted the need for server and server-related communications and activities like aggregation etc.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Similar results can be seen in Figures <a href="#S7.F5.sf1" title="In Fig. 5 ‣ VII-B Results. ‣ VII Experiment ‣ orb-QFL: Orbital Quantum Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a> and <a href="#S7.F5.sf2" title="In Fig. 5 ‣ VII-B Results. ‣ VII Experiment ‣ orb-QFL: Orbital Quantum Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(b)</span></a> in terms of devices’ performance.
The overall average accuracy for all devices in terms of training is consistently better with orb-QFL as shown in Figure <a href="#S7.F5.sf1" title="In Fig. 5 ‣ VII-B Results. ‣ VII Experiment ‣ orb-QFL: Orbital Quantum Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a>. While in testing, the performance is again slightly improved with orb-QFL as shown in Figure <a href="#S7.F5.sf2" title="In Fig. 5 ‣ VII-B Results. ‣ VII Experiment ‣ orb-QFL: Orbital Quantum Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(b)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S7.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.16505v1/figures/server_test_acc.png" id="S7.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/476;" width="476" height="476" alt="Refer to caption">
